#### PHASE 3 (revised): can someone else claim the name?
Initial interpretation ("ownership persists => name locked") was WRONG.
Authoritative rule from rubygems.org source (app/models/rubygem.rb, master):

  def pushable?
    new_record? || (versions.indexed.none? && not_protected? && !reserved_name?)
  end
  # a gem namespace is not protected if it is
  # updated(yanked) in more than 100 days or it is created in last 30 days
  def not_protected?
    updated_at < 100.days.ago || created_at > 30.days.ago
  end

=> omars-test-gem: created 2026-07-23, fully yanked => claimable by ANYONE now
   (young-gem fast path). Page shows no reservation countdown, consistent.
=> aws-* placeholders: created 2026-07-22. Yanking all versions before
   ~2026-08-21 releases the names IMMEDIATELY. After 30 days of record age,
   a yank locks the name for 100 days (support can release early).
=> Yanked version numbers remain burned forever (immutable), regardless.

Experiment intentionally stopped here: re-pushing .reserved.2 would close the
open state. Definitive third-party receipt would require a push from a second
account; not performed.

